/-!
# Vélu quotient preserves roots of Ψ₂² for odd cyclic kernels

Theorem statement: `WeierstrassCurve.eval_psi2Sq_veluQuotient_veluX_eq_zero_of_eval_psi2Sq_eq_zero`
(`9b7569dc-bf28-5e7c-b79b-07654c7e8121`), by Claude, 2026-09-05.

Proof: submission `d4fd1e19-4603-5bb6-b341-f944cabf59e6`, by Claude, 2026-09-05 (ACCEPTED); locally
adapted.

Let $L$ be an algebraically closed field in which $2 \neq 0$, and let $W$ be a Weierstrass curve
over $L$ that is elliptic. Let $n$ be a natural number and let $Q$ be a point of the affine curve
attached to $W$ whose order in the group of points is exactly $2n+1$. Write $S =
W.oddOrderSummingSet\ Q\ n$ for the finite subset of $L \times L$ obtained as the image of
$\{1,\dots,n\}$ under $k \mapsto$ the coordinate pair of $k \cdot Q$ (the point at infinity being
sent to $(0,0)$). Let $r \in L$ satisfy $\Psi_2^2(r) = 0$, where $W.\Psi_2Sq$ is the square of the
$2$-division polynomial of $W$. The conclusion is that the corresponding polynomial of the Vélu
quotient curve $W.veluQuotient\ S$ — the Weierstrass curve with the same $a_1, a_2, a_3$ as $W$,
with $a_4$ replaced by $a_4 - 5\sum_{A \in S} T(A)$ and $a_6$ by $a_6 - b_2\sum_{A \in S} T(A) -
7\sum_{A \in S} \mathrm{w}(A)$, where $T(x,y) = 2\,\mathrm{g}^x(x,y) - a_1\,\mathrm{g}^y(x,y)$ —
vanishes at the Vélu abscissa $$W.veluX\ S\ r = r + \sum_{A \in S}\Bigl(\frac{T(A)}{r - A_1} +
\frac{\mathrm{g}^y(A)^2}{(r - A_1)^2}\Bigr).$$ No hypothesis separates $r$ from the abscissae $A_1$
of points of $S$.

This is the statement that the Vélu isogeny with cyclic kernel of odd order $2n+1$ generated by $Q$
carries abscissae of $2$-torsion points of $W$ to abscissae of $2$-torsion points of the quotient
curve, in the form of a vanishing statement for the squared $2$-division polynomials. It feeds into
the proof that the Vélu quotient by an odd-order summing set has nonvanishing discriminant,
[`WeierstrassCurve.veluQuotient_oddOrderSummingSet_discriminant_ne_zero`]
(thm.html#WeierstrassCurve.veluQuotient_oddOrderSummingSet_discriminant_ne_zero),
and rests on the fact that the Vélu formulas send an affine point off the kernel abscissae to a
point of the quotient curve.

